INT. THE HARGREAVES KITCHEN - NIGHT

Rain against the window, steady and unremarkable. The fridge hum has died. Somewhere down the road a transformer has given up, and the whole street has gone the colour of weak tea.

A single candle on the table. A cold pot of something on the hob. A mug, a second mug, a phone face-up between them.

ROSALIND HARGREAVES, 50s, cardigan, reading glasses on a string, stands at the sink with her back to the room. Her son, TOM, 17, sits at the table in a school blazer he has not taken off.

The clock on the wall has stopped at ten past nine.

ROSALIND
(rinsing a cup that is already clean)
You're back early.

TOM
(turning the phone over, then back)
Not really.

Outside, a dog barks twice and thinks better of it.

ON PHONE: "DAD: Spoke to your mum. We'll sort it. Don't be daft."

ROSALIND
(without turning)
He texted you as well, did he?

TOM
(a very small nod)
Said not to be daft.

ROSALIND
(drying her hands slowly on a tea towel)
That does sound like him.

The candle gutters. A gust finds the back door and goes away again. Neither of them moves to fix it.

TOM
(picking at the edge of the table)
I got the letter from Durham.

ROSALIND
(her shoulders very slightly tightening)
Did you.

TOM
(a breath out through his nose)
They've offered me a place.

ROSALIND
(finally turning, the towel in both hands)
Well. That's marvellous.

TOM
(not looking up)
You don't sound it.

ROSALIND
(a small, brittle laugh)
I'm told it's the done thing, to sound it.

The rain eases for a moment. In the quiet, the house ticks as it cools.

This is the last evening the three of them will pretend to be a family under this roof.

TOM
(standing, the chair scraping the tiles)
I've not even said I'll go yet.

ROSALIND
(folding the towel, setting it down, squaring its corners)
You've said it. You said it when you came in with your coat still on.

ON PHONE: "DAD: pick up. please."

TOM
(snatching the phone, thumb over the screen, a crack in his voice)
Mum, I'm not leaving you here on your own.

ROSALIND
(crossing the kitchen, taking the phone from his hand, setting it face-down on the table)
Thomas. You will go. And I shall be perfectly all right. It's only a power cut.

The candle burns on. Somewhere across the road, a generator coughs and catches, and one lit window blinks into being.
